  "query_preview": "..\n\nSYNOPTIC SETUP:\n\nA low pressure system over Idaho this morning will track east \nacross southern Montana and northern Wyoming tonight and Sunday. \nThis system is cutoff by a northern stream shallow ridge in the\nCanadian Prairies which is leading to dry air cutting off the \nnorthern extent of the precipitation north of the Missouri River. \nLatest WPC, EC and CMC ensembles forecast QPF shows a sharp \ngradient along the Missouri River with little or no precipitation \nnorth of the Missouri River and over an inch south of MT 200 and \nMT 200S through Winnett, Jordan, and Glendive and south of I-94 \nbetween Glendive and Wibaux. Across these southern locations, \nheavier bands of showers and embedded thunderstorms that remain \npersistent enough may produce minor flooding of small streams, \nopen fields, and poor drainage locations such as low lying areas.\nA Flood Watch has been issued for Petroleum, Garfield, Prairie,\nDawson and Wibaux Counties today through Sunday. \n\nAt this time, confidence has increased in that heavy rain is\nlikely south and little or no rain is expected north. However, exact\nprecipitation totals through Sunday remains below average in the \nsharp gradient between the Missouri River and MT 200. The \nprobability of one inch of rain is 60 to 80 percent south of MT \n200 and drops quickly to less than 10 percent to the north.\n\nConfidence is greater to the south of the CWA closer to the storm\ntrack. The WPC now has a Moderate Risk for Excessive Rainfall \njust south of the CWA for Day 1 with a Slight Risk South of MT \n200. \n\nThe storm system looks to exit Sunday into Monday with ensembles\nshowing the northern stream ridge having a bit more influence over\nconditions. As a result look for a warm up in temperatures and\ndrier weather into early next week.\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS:\n\nConfidence is high that there will be heavy rain in most areas\nsouth of MT 200 and little or no rain north of the Missouri River.\nConfidence is low in rainfall totals in between due to the sharp\ngradient between the Missouri River and MT 200. Minor tweaks to\npops at times today through Sunday with no other changes to NBM.",
